      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186179719,
          "id": "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095",
          "title": "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "d15e5e12-b86a-5b2f-bb75-5f58197f11de",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- x + y$ at the points $(-2,-1)$, $(1,2)$, $(1,1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x + y\\right)=-1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x + y\\right)=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(-1\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(1\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,-1)&=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-5ek4tgt5/17592186179719/a41699b2aab47fdeeb451be02915468a5c3ca805f55a46c861a8145a6c3139ec.png)",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
