        "question": {
          ":question/id": "9e583ef3-362c-5b35-be49-065ded039c64",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}$ at the points $(-2,-1)$, $(1,2)$, $(1,1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=- x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(- x\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,-1)&=\\langle (-1)(-2),\\;(1)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle (-1)(1),\\;(1)(2)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle (-1)(1),\\;(1)(1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-5ek4tgt5/17592186179719/6f8e9a6353b66b9c5ce38dbb4309a73a724a589baea8191a9de6a47e6a3a32f1.png)",
